Prepared for the purpose of obtaining and giving legal advice. Do not forward.

Dear Chair,

Yesterday, 3 September 2026, the Society received a preliminary enforcement notice from the Cooperative Prudential Board, reference CPB/ENF/2026/0193. This note sets out what it says, our options and what I need from the Board.

1. What the regulator alleges

The notice concerns late external suspicion reports. Our procedure requires an external report to be filed within 9 working days of the internal report. The regulator's review found that, between 1 August 2025 and 31 January 2026, 41 of the 167 external reports we filed were late. The longest delay was 34 working days. The regulator accepts that no report was withheld and that we reported the problem ourselves on 12 February 2026.

2. The cause

The delays followed a vacancy in the compliance team from July 2025 and a backlog during the changeover of the Money Laundering Reporting Officer in October 2024, which was never fully cleared. The backlog was cleared by 27 February 2026 and no report has been late since.

3. The proposed penalty

The regulator proposes a financial penalty of $236,000 and a public statement. If we settle at the first stage, without contesting the findings, the penalty is reduced by 27.5%, which brings it to $171,100. The public statement would still be made but we would see the draft 4 working days beforehand.

4. The deadline

We must respond within 19 working days of receiving the notice, that is by 30 September 2026. A response may accept the findings, contest them or ask for an oral hearing.

5. Advice

I have instructed Harrowgate & Pym as external counsel, with fees capped at $74,500. Their preliminary view, which I share, is:

- the findings of fact are accurate and would be hard to contest;
- our self-report and the speed of the fix are strong mitigation and are already reflected in the proposed figure;
- contesting would be likely to cost more than the 27.5% discount is worth ($64,900) and would lose it;
- we should settle at the first stage, and ask that the public statement record that members suffered no loss.

6. What I need from the Board

The decision to settle is reserved to the Board. I ask that it be taken at the Board's fifth ordinary meeting of 2026, which falls before the deadline. I will brief the Risk Committee at its third meeting of 2026 beforehand. A provision of $171,100 plus the legal fee cap should be made in the September management accounts; the Chief Financial Officer is aware.

7. Confidentiality

The notice is confidential until the regulator publishes its final statement. Disclosure before then could itself be a breach. Please do not discuss it outside the Board, and please keep it separate from the other restricted matter currently before the Board, as the advisers on that matter have not been told about the notice. I will tell them once the Board has decided, since it will be relevant to their due diligence disclosures.

8. Individuals

The notice does not propose action against any individual. The regulator has asked to interview the Money Laundering Reporting Officer and the Chief Risk Officer in the week of 14 September 2026. External counsel will attend both interviews.

I am available all day today if you would like to talk it through.

